%% What it shows: what LangGraph does with Command(resume=...) on a thread id. If the checkpointer holds a paused
%% run for that thread, the paused node runs again and interrupt() returns the decision. If it holds nothing, a new
%% run starts at START with an empty state, and no error is raised.
%% Ink: what the model does. Lime: what our own code does, or the step to look at.
%% Export: npx -y @mermaid-js/mermaid-cli -i resume_on_a_thread.mmd -o resume_on_a_thread.png -b white -s 2
flowchart LR
    call["our code<br/>invoke(Command(resume=...), config)"]:::code --> saver[(checkpointer:<br/>looks up the<br/>thread id)]
    saver --> has{a paused run<br/>on this thread?}
    has -->|yes| node["the paused node runs again;<br/>interrupt() returns the decision"]
    node --> go([the run continues<br/>where it paused])
    has -->|no| fresh["a new run starts at START<br/>with an empty state;<br/>no error"]:::code
    fresh --> model["model node:<br/>called with the system prompt<br/>and no messages"]:::model
    classDef model fill:#0B1220,color:#FFFFFF,stroke:#0B1220
    classDef code fill:#C9F24D,color:#0B1220,stroke:#0B1220
